    "result_text": "The full suite (one test class) is green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the direct level rule with a points system.\n\n**New behavior** \u2014 points start at 0, +2 email present, +1 `namesakeCount == 0`, +2 household of 3+, +3 tenure over 365 days; level maps 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+). Both `membershipPoints` and `membershipLevel` are returned.\n\n**Changes (kept structurally small):**\n- `util/Membership.java` (new) \u2014 two tiny pure functions, `points(owner, largeHousehold, longTenure)` and `level(points)`. All the new logic lives here in a class with almost no surrounding complexity, so it's cheap to change.\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 swapped the old inline `membershipLevel` expression for two one-line expressions that delegate to `Membership` (base factors: email + namesake), so every `OwnerDto` carries both fields consistently.\n- `controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 in `getOwner`, replaced the old 3-line \"bump to level 4\" block with a compute-once call that folds in the real household-size and tenure flags and sets both fields. Net complexity of that method went *down* (the compound `if` is gone).\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` schema property (generates the DTO getter/setter) and updated the `membershipLevel` description.\n\nThe household/tenure factors need the owners collection, which only exists in the controller, so the mapper computes the base and `getOwner` enriches it \u2014 no change to any of the large controller's other methods.",
